Third-party exam material
-------------------------
AMC 10/12 problems are copyright MAA. AP questions are copyright College Board. Neither is included
here, and the MIT licence cannot cover them. If you keep your own copies for personal practice, put
them in `content/local/`: that directory and the file generated from it (`public/local-deck.json`) are
gitignored, loaded only at runtime on your machine, and never published. Local items are the one part
of the bank this repository did not write and cannot vouch for — they are labelled 本地题库
(local bank), always marked unverified, and may carry an optional `[难度 1-4]` marker graded against
the same rubric as the built-in items. See docs/local-bank.md.

AI-generated content
--------------------
Everything in the built-in bank is AI-generated: the pack's 原创 items by its own documentation, and
everything written here — concept-card rewrites, the 40 added drill items, all 262 scenario items and
all 1466 English entries — by an AI agent working in this repository. The app labels provenance on
every card and repeats it on the practice screen. Treat the bank as review material, not as exam
paper.

"Verified" means: the answer was put through an independent recomputation pass — on 2026-09-19,
sixteen sharded AI passes with hand recomputation of every flagged item, plus human-driven spot
checks — and the card's current text still hashes to the version that was checked
(scripts/flashcard-verification.json: 1466 registered, 1466 verified as of 2026-10-18). It does not
mean a human expert read the card, and editing a card's text silently returns it to unverified until
somebody reworks it. Local items are marked unverified because this repository has not checked them.

The English overlay
-------------------
The overlay is synchronised with the Chinese by scripts, and the guarantee is narrower than "the
English says the same thing":

- Structural equality is gated. `check-flashcards` requires four option letters, the same keyed
  answer letter as the Chinese, a `Correct option` that matches the option it names character for
  character, no leftover Chinese characters, no orphan ids, and a `src` hash of the Chinese front+back
  that the English was translated from. Editing the Chinese without re-running
  `node scripts/sync-overlays.mjs --write` fails the gate. Scenario items must be covered in full; a
  missing entry on a concept or drill card is reported as a fraction rather than failing.
- Option *order* across the two languages is only partly machine-checkable. `sync-overlays.mjs
  --check` compares English option lines to Chinese ones by a language-neutral fingerprint (numbers,
  and since 2026-10-18 also identifiers such as dotted paths, camel/Pascal names, `name(` forms,
  ALL_CAPS, `int[]` and a closed list of primitive and JDK types). Over the 902 option cards it
  decides 660 — 304 outright, where the four fingerprints are distinct and the two multisets match,
  and another 356 whose fingerprint sequences already agree position by position, which is not a
  proof but is the order being right. 20 cards have fingerprints on both sides that do not line up and
  are listed by id for a person to read. The remaining 222 have neither a digit nor an identifier
  among one side's four options: the fingerprint carries no information there, so the machine can
  neither prove nor flag the order and only a human comparing the two languages can settle it.
- The gate validates each language against itself. A swap between two options that share an empty
  fingerprint, with `Answer:` and `Correct option` moved along, is invisible to every check in this
  repository — that is the residual 222 above, not a claim that they are wrong.
- Five cards (`precalc-scenario-033/034/040/041/042`) cannot be ordered by the fingerprint at all and
  are ordered by the explicit permutation in scripts/overlay-order-overrides.json.

So: the Chinese and English are kept in step structurally by tooling, and the option order is decided
for 660 of the 902 option cards (proven for 304, already-agreeing for 356). Nothing here proves the
*meaning* of a translation, and for the other 242 cards nothing proves the order either; the overlays
remain AI translations that have been read in part, not certified.

Difficulty annotations
----------------------
Levels 1–4 on the 902 option-bearing cards are a rubric judgement about how much work an item asks
for, not measured difficulty: nobody has answered these items, there is no response data behind a
level, and the figures above are the label distribution, not a difficulty result. The rubric, worked
examples at every level, the known failure modes and the re-grade procedure are in
docs/difficulty-rubric.md, with the per-item judgement in content/subject-banks/difficulty.json. The
564 concept cards are outside this scale and use their own core / advance / boundary tags, which are
a coverage classification rather than a difficulty measurement. A local item's `[难度 n]` marker is
the user's own judgement, copied from the same rubric by the user, and never checked here.
